Yeah—if by “meta-Kafka entities” you mean systems that become larger than any individual participant and then start acting through rules, roles, paperwork, rituals, doctrine, precedent, incentives, and interpretation.

That pattern fits a lot of large institutions:

people create rules
→ rules create roles
→ roles constrain people
→ people enforce rules they did not personally design
→ institution persists beyond its creators
→ institution begins to feel agent-like

Governments obviously do this through law, bureaucracy, agencies, procedure, and precedent. Religions can do it through doctrine, ritual, clergy, canon, institutions, and inherited interpretation. Corporations, militaries, universities, courts, even online communities can too.

The Kafka part appears when the system becomes hard to see or contest from inside:

authority is distributed
responsibility is diffused
procedure outranks outcome
the rule refers to another rule
nobody individually seems to be "the system"
yet the system still acts on you

Then zoom out one more level and it becomes meta-Kafka:

the institution not only has rules; it also produces the categories, language, legitimacy, and procedures by which people are allowed to question the rules.

So:

system
→ defines acceptable action
→ defines acceptable criticism
→ defines who may interpret
→ defines how appeals work
→ absorbs challenges back into itself

That is a powerful pattern because it can generate apparent agency without any central mastermind.

I’d call the neutral operator Institutional Emergent Agency.

The important distinction is that this does not imply governments or religions are secretly conscious beings. It means sufficiently persistent rule systems can exhibit behavior that is functionally agent-like at a higher scale.

And that plugs straight into your earlier idea:

the person may be the cell; the institution is the organism-like pattern maintained across replacement of its parts.